DefinitionSliding window memory keeps only the most recent N messages in a conversation, discarding older ones to stay within context limits.Why it is usedPrevents context overflowControls token usageKeeps relevant recent conversationImproves performance and costHow it works (step-by-step)Store incoming messages in memoryDefine window size (N messages or tokens)When new message arrives:Add it to memoryRemove oldest messages if limit exceededReturn current window as contextKey componentsWindow size: Max messages or tokensMessage buffer: Stores conversationEviction policy: Remove oldest entriesBasic implementation (Python - message-based)class SlidingWindowMemory:
    def __init__(self, max_messages=5):
        self.max_messages = max_messages
        self.messages = []

    def add(self, role, content):
        self.messages.append({"role": role, "content": content})

        # Remove oldest if exceeds limit
        if len(self.messages) > self.max_messages:
            self.messages.pop(0)

    def get_context(self):
        return self.messages


